# happy (slopus/happy) retired in #331 / PR #333 — deliberately NOT listed here, unlike dmux
# above.
# happy owned no deployed files of its own (it lived as lines inside claude.zsh/codex.zsh/
# mise config), and its runtime dir ~/.happy holds phone-pairing and E2E key material
# rather than dmux's regenerable socket dir. A standing per-apply deletion would therefore
# wipe those keys with no warning if happy were ever reinstalled. tests/files.bats asserts
# that `.happy` stays absent from this file; removing that assertion is the deliberate way
# to reverse this decision.
#
# The cleanup is a manual, ORDER-DEPENDENT procedure instead. Recorded here rather than in
# the PR description so it survives where the guard test points:
#   1. happy daemon stop            # stop first: a live daemon rewrites state and recreates the dir
#      happy doctor clean           # the CLI's own catch-all for leftover happy processes
#      pgrep -f 'happy/dist/index\.mjs daemon'   # confirm empty
#   2. Unpair/remove this device in the Happy mobile/web app BEFORE step 3. Deleting the
#      local access.key is not revocation — any backup copy stays a valid credential.
#   3. rm -rf ~/.happy
#   4. mise uninstall npm:happy@<version>   # surgical; bare `mise prune` spans every tool
#      rm -f ~/.local/share/mise/shims/happy ~/.local/share/mise/shims/happy-mcp
#      (plain `mise reshim` only ADDS shims — it does not remove orphaned ones)
# Note: ~/.happy/logs holds sessions from BOTH accounts (the pairing was shared), so this
# is a data-classification cleanup, not just a disk-space one. On APFS do not chase a
# secure erase (srm is gone, `rm -P` is meaningless on SSD) — FileVault is the real control.

